#!/usr/bin/perl
# lzrprf: owns the one window of an application, runs the application inside a
# container that this file defines, and does for it the one thing it may have
# done: fetch the https address that you typed into the bar of that window.
#
#   lzrprf run /path/to/program [arguments]     open the window, with the program contained behind it
#   lzrprf log                                  print what the log on this computer holds
#   lzrprf keep [nothing|hosts|all [DAYS]]      say, or choose, what is kept after a window closes
#   lzrprf address TEXT...                      say what would be fetched if TEXT were typed into the bar
#   lzrprf request TEXT...                      print the exact bytes a site is sent for TEXT
#   lzrprf public NUMBER...                     say whether a numeric address is on the public internet, and how this computer would send to it
#   lzrprf mark                                 this build of macOS, this file and the selftest beside it (each file by its SHA-256), as one line; an empty line when no selftest can be read beside it
#
# The application is treated as hostile. It has no window, no network, no
# file of yours and no service of the system: it is started once for each answer
# lzrprf has already fetched, reads that answer, and writes a list of what to
# draw. lzrprf checks every line of the list and draws it. The window, its
# address bar, its buttons and its words are lzrprf's, made by the text at the
# end of this file.
#
# Three kinds of process are started here, each inside a container:
#   the PAGE container holds the application, once for each answer;
#   the FETCH container holds the system's own curl, once for each request;
#   the WINDOW container holds the window.
# What is outside every container is this file: it looks up the name, makes
# the one connection, writes the log, and checks what crosses.
#
# THE CONDITIONS. Each promise lzrprf makes is a condition in this file, and
# the line that holds it carries its mark. The README says the same in words
# and cites these marks, and selftest fails when a mark is cited that is not
# here, or the other way round.
#
#   [C1]  The application is started in one place only, and only inside the page container.
#   [C2]  At every start lzrprf tries, from inside that same container, what the container must refuse, and starts nothing unless all of it was refused and the same tries worked outside.
#   [C3]  No process is handed a byte of what was fetched, nor the connection to a site, before the system itself says that this process is contained. Before that, of what you asked for each knows the address alone: the application in one variable, curl among its fixed switches.
#   [C4]  A request is made only for an address that came from the window's bar, or for one of two fixed addresses after you said yes, or for a picture that the page laid out from such an address names on that same host, at most eight of them, by a path lzrprf checked. There is no fourth way to a request.
#   [C5]  Nothing is looked up or fetched before its line is in the log: in the memory of the process behind the window, and on the disk too when you chose to keep a log. No answer is shown whose line could not be recorded.
#   [C6]  There is one place that connects to a site, and it connects to a numeric address that was checked and written down.
#   [C7]  What a site is sent is made from the address alone: the same request from every computer, byte for byte, with nothing kept from an earlier request.
#   [C8]  A host inside your own network is fetched only after you said yes to exactly that, once.
#   [C9]  Every line the application writes is checked before the window is given it, and what is not one of five kinds of line, or a laid page, is not drawn at all.
#   [C10] The window's process has no network of its own, writes no file, starts no other program, opens nothing through the system, commands no other program, sends no signal to another process, and reads nothing under /Users or on another disk but your settings, fonts, keyboard layouts and input methods. Before the window takes an address or shows a fetched page, lzrprf asks the system about eight operations of that process (connecting out, creating a file, starting a second process, reaching a system service when none is named, opening something through the system, commanding another program, writing your settings, sending a signal), and each must be refused; the rest of that container's text, its list of services among it, is not asked about, and selftest makes its own tries from the real window. Of the system's services it looks up by name only the 40 its container lists and one local name, the clipboard's.
#   [C11] No page is fetched or shown before selftest held on this build of macOS with this file and the selftest beside it, in its last run there. The window is there earlier, with its bar switched off: it shows lzrprf's own questions and no page. A check the test could not try there is named in its mark, and then no page is fetched or shown before you said yes to starting without exactly those checks.
#   [C12] Nothing of what you fetched stays on the disk unless you chose a kept log; a kept log holds hosts only unless you chose full addresses. It is only added to, apart from clearing by your button and the days you chose: in a kept log each of the two says so in its first line, and with nothing kept, clearing removes the log whole and the window's lines say so.
#
# This file is the whole program. Its parts, in order: the containers, the
# proof that they hold, one visit, the fetch, the window's side, the log, the
# window itself.
use strict;
use warnings;
use Cwd 'abs_path';
use Digest::SHA;
use Fcntl qw(:flock O_RDONLY O_RDWR O_WRONLY O_APPEND O_CREAT O_EXCL O_NOFOLLOW O_NONBLOCK);
